# Architecture

> The shape of {{PROJECT_NAME}}: context, containers, components and the rules
> that hold them together.

## 1. System context

{{PROJECT_NAME}} is {{SYSTEM_PURPOSE}}. It is used by {{ACTORS}} and depends on
{{EXTERNAL_SYSTEMS}}.

Diagram: `docs/planning/c4/context.puml`.

| External system | Purpose | Failure behavior |
| --------------- | ------- | ---------------- |
| {{SYSTEM}}      | {{WHY}} | {{WHAT_HAPPENS_WHEN_DOWN}} |

## 2. Containers

Diagram: `docs/planning/c4/container.puml`.

| Container     | Technology | Responsibility            |
| ------------- | ---------- | ------------------------- |
| {{CONTAINER}} | {{STACK}}  | {{RESPONSIBILITY}}        |

Rules:

- A container owns its data; no container reaches into another's database.
- Synchronous calls have a timeout and a documented failure mode.
- Cross-container contracts live in `docs/planning/contracts.md`.

## 3. Components

Diagram: `docs/planning/c4/component.puml`.

The application is split into layers. Dependencies flow inward.

| Layer            | Contains                       | May import from          |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------ |
| {{LAYER}}        | {{CONTENTS}}                   | {{ALLOWED_DEPENDENCIES}} |

Rules:

- No cycles between layers.
- Domain logic does not import transport or persistence frameworks.
- I/O lives at the boundary, not inside pure logic.
- A new layer needs an ADR.

## 4. Data

{{DATA_STORES}}

- Every persisted entity has an explicit schema and a migration policy.
- Migrations are forward-only and reviewed with the story that introduces them.
- No production data in tests; use fixtures.

## 5. Cross-cutting concerns

{{CROSS_CUTTING}}

Consider, at minimum: configuration, secrets, logging, error taxonomy,
observability, authentication, authorization, rate limits and backups. Write the
decision or link the ADR that holds it.

## 6. Decisions

Architecture decisions live in `docs/planning/adr/`. This document describes the
current state; the ADRs record why it changed. If a story changes the
architecture, it must update this document and add an ADR in the same activity.
